.TH KFS 4
.SH NAME
kfs \- Vectra's own file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kfs
[
.B \-r
]
.I device
[
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kfs
serves a filesystem of Vectra's own on
.IR device ,
keeping what
.IR fatfs (4)
cannot: an owner-free but real set of permission bits, a qid version that
moves on every write, and files past FAT32's ceiling, up to a little over
four megabytes (see
.IR kfs (6)
for the shape on the disk).
It posts
.I srvname
.RB ( /srv/kfs
when not given), then forks: the parent exits once the name is there, and
the child serves until the last mount and the name are both gone.
.PP
The
.B \-r
flag reams
.I device
first: it lays a fresh, empty volume down over whatever was there, with one
directory,
.BR glenda ,
in the root.
Without
.BR \-r ,
.I device
must already hold a volume
.I kfs
reams itself, once, if it does not, with a line on the console saying so --
a blank scratch disk is expected the first time a machine boots, and a boot
that stopped over it would not be one anyone wanted.
.PP
The boot mounts the machine's second disk this way, at
.B /usr
(see
.IR intro (1)),
and
.B $home
is
.BR /usr/glenda .
.PP
Every file has Plan 9's permission bits and a directory bit, kept as
given; there is no owner and no group, so the bits apply to everyone alike.
A file's qid version is the inode's, which counts every write, so a
client that cached the file can tell it changed.
Every change reaches the disk as it is made, in the order that leaves the
volume consistent if the machine stops between any two writes.
.PP
The server stops on the pipe's hang-up, when the last mount and the posted
name are both gone.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /srv/ srvname
the posted service,
.B kfs
by default
.TP
.B /usr/glenda
home, once mounted
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/kfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR fork (2),
.IR getattr (5),
.IR fatfs (4),
.IR kfs (6),
.IR sd (3)
.SH BUGS
There is no rename.
.I Cp
and
.I mv
(see
.IR cp (1))
copy and remove instead.
A file cannot pass 4243456 bytes, twelve direct blocks and one indirect
block of 1024 more, at 4096 bytes each; a second level of indirection
would raise it and does not exist yet.
